## DESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Summary statistics)
## Institution(The College of Idaho)
## Author(R Cruz, L Danielson, J Guild)
## MLT(stat-des-mean-median)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('The Basic Practice of Statistics')
## AuthorText1('David Moore')
## EditionText1('5e')
## Section1('2')
## KEYWORDS('statistics', 'mean', 'median')

DOCUMENT();    # This should be the first executable line in the problem.

loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGchoicemacros.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

@data         = map { random(10, 60) } (1 .. 8);
@ordered_data = num_sort(@data);

$sum = 0;
$sum += $_ for (@data);
$mean   = $sum / scalar(@data);
$median = 0.5 * ($ordered_data[3] + $ordered_data[4]);

BEGIN_PGML
Calculate the mean and median of the following data: 
[``` [@ join(', ', @data) @] ```]

a) Mean = [_]{$mean}

b) Median = [_]{$median}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) First find the sum of all of the data or [$sum].

    The mean is [`` \frac{[$sum]}{8} = [$mean] ``]

b) To find the median, first order the data from smallest to largest or 

    [``` [@ join(', ', @ordered_data) @] ```]

    Since there is an even number of data points, the median is the average of the middle two or

    median: [`` \frac{[$ordered_data[3]]+[$ordered_data[4]]}{2} = [$median] ``]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
